:: StoryInterface
<!--
	Story chrome — two display modes around the story's single #passages
	element. RAW HTML: SugarCube wraps this content in its own #story element
	(authoring another one here is fatal) and does not process macros.
	SugarCube 2.37 requires exactly one #passages inside a custom
	StoryInterface — this is the story's one and only; every passage renders
	inside it.

	Shell mode (default; the title and any non-checker passage): the compact
	rail panel beside a centered reading card (shell.css).

	Stage mode ([checker] passages that are not [title]; derived from the passage
	tag classes SugarCube puts on <body> and published as body[data-stage-mode]):
	the letterboxed .stage frame
	(stage.css), aspect-locked to the 2840:1700 design canvas. The slots:
	the background — #tmem-bg, the existing animated checkerboard, declared
	here and letterboxed into the same box by checker.css;
	the four content slots below are empty until the content macros fill them —
	Cam's portrait, Cam's speech-bubble, the system card and the player's
	reply bar; a layout composes only the ones it states;
	and the menu — the same .rail, repositioned by stage.css into the stage's
	menu slot.

	The rail is a sibling of .shell rather than a child, so one element serves
	as the shell's rail and the stage's menu. It is position: fixed in both
	modes, and stage.css repositions it.

	Wiring: rail links carry data-shell-action and are handled in shell.js,
	which resolves SugarCube's API at click time. Inline onclick cannot work
	here: SugarCube 2.37.3 exposes UI / Engine / State / Story on
	window.SugarCube only — there are no bare globals to reach from an inline
	handler.
-->
<!-- first child of the StoryInterface: the background layer, letterboxed
     into the stage box by checker.css. Declared here rather than built by JS
     so the page has a background on first paint — it is the only page
     background in shell mode — and so the pattern is markup a redesign can
     edit. -->
<div id="tmem-bg"><div class="pattern"></div></div>
<!-- The background track: two looping media elements that persist across
     passages, because a track change crossfades one into the other. music.js
     owns their sources, playback and volumes; declaring them here keeps the
     page's persistent surfaces in markup and gives the page a fixed pair of
     audio elements rather than one fabricated per track. Either one can carry
     the current track. No autoplay attribute: the module starts it on the
     page's first gesture when the browser requires one. -->
<audio id="music" loop preload="auto"></audio>
<audio id="music-b" loop preload="auto"></audio>
<div class="shell">
	<div class="main">
		<div id="passages" class="pixel-frame reading-card"></div>
	</div>
</div>
<aside class="rail pixel-frame">
	<nav class="rail-actions">
		<a href="#" title="Settings" data-shell-action="settings">Settings</a>
		<a href="#" title="Saves" data-shell-action="saves">Saves</a>
		<a href="#" title="Restart" data-shell-action="restart">Restart</a>
	</nav>
</aside>
<div class="stage stage-window">
	<div class="stage-slot stage-cam pixel-frame" data-stage-slot="cam" data-slot-label="Cam portrait"></div>
	<!-- The bubble slot owns its surface: the speech nine-patch is drawn on
	     .stage-bubble::before by the Pixel frame's paper variant (frame.css),
	     and stage.js writes the line into an inner .stage-bubble-prose box, so
	     the paper stays put while the prose scrolls. The pointer tail is the
	     slot's ::after, placed per layout. -->
	<div class="stage-slot stage-bubble pixel-frame-paper" data-stage-slot="bubble" data-slot-label="Cam speech-bubble"></div>
	<!-- The system card: the title screen's reading card in the speech bubble's
	     box, for Layout 4's pre-game message. stage.js writes into an inner
	     .reading-card-prose box, so the frame stays put while a long message
	     scrolls. Empty draws nothing, and every layout but 4 hides it. -->
	<div class="stage-slot stage-system pixel-frame reading-card" data-stage-slot="system" data-slot-label="System card"></div>
	<div class="stage-slot stage-reply pixel-frame" data-stage-slot="reply" data-slot-label="Player reply bar"></div>
</div>
<!-- Cam's hand, for a pose that is drawn in two halves. Outside the frame
     because a fixed box is a stacking context, and the hand has to reach over
     the menu. Same window as the frame, same design pixels: stage.css states
     the box once, for .stage-cam and .stage-hand both. stage.js fills it only
     for a pose that has a hand, so every other pose leaves it empty. -->
<div class="stage-cam-hand stage-window"></div>